From 924fd55a53c58eae769e301cb0dd1d07ce2f85b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jd1100 <248791769+ayooooo123@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 22:48:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] feat(private): authenticated production NAT traversal closing
 KI-13

The punch that makes a dispatch work was in the harness, not in
production (KI-13). This moves it into lib/private with the
authorization the protocol demands, and states plainly what a punch
buys: connectivity evidence, never identity.

Production owns the reflected address:
- lib/private/nat-reflect.js reflects the endpoint's own socket off
  the DHT bootstraps through dht-exit-wire PINGs. Only two-reflector
  agreement is publishable; disagreement means no publishable value.
- dht-exit-wire gains createDhtExitPingReservation and
  observeDhtExitPingReply: the reply decoder reads `to` WITHOUT the
  local-tuple equality check (the tuple `to` names IS the answer)
  while still binding the reply to the expected responder and tid.

The punch plan is a signed artifact, not a harness peer list:
- lib/private/nat-punch.js encodes both peer identities, both
  published tuples, epoch, run id, validity window and plan id into a
  canonical encoding signed by the topology authority over a new
  NAT_PUNCH_PLAN domain. verifyNatPunchPlan returns the caller's own
  side. A wrong signature, a stranger identity, a wrong advertised
  address, an expired window or a tampered byte each fail closed
  before a datagram is sent.

The endpoint punches on its own socket:
- UdxCellEndpoint.punchPeer runs the six-round simultaneous send from
  the bound port; every udx send Promise<boolean> is observed and
  counted, no timer outlives the round, stop() resolves waiters so
  close() cannot hang, and close() stops a live round.
- UdxCellEndpoint.punchPlan verifies the plan, pins it to the
  endpoint's advertised pair, limits accepted plans, and reports
  sent/refused/received/strayReceived.
- Punch-tagged datagrams are counted only from the verified peer
  tuple; anything else is stray. A punch never advances a bootstrap
  session or substitutes for a link grant.
- dispatchLinkedPacket routes replies to a PENDING initiate session,
  and to an accept session only with the explicit acceptDirect opt-in
  (the punched-link responder has no bootstrap admission). Every
  other path keeps the existing onBootstrap contract.

The link path is wired:
- LinkBootstrapSession.open({ punch }) joins the caller's round: the
  first CREATE dispatches only after the round settles, and the round
  is stopped when the session fails, closes or is tombstoned.
- The projected link service gains punchPlanFor(encoding), verifying
  against the authority it already pins and running the round on the
  role's own endpoint.

Tests (aggregate suite 1029/1029, 19216/19216):
- test/private/nat-punch.js: canonical ordering, both-side views,
  wrong signature/identity/window/tamper/codec rejects.
- test/private/nat-traversal-live.js: two real UdxCellEndpoints on
  distinct tuples, no pre-opened path, one signed plan driving the
  simultaneous round, stray punch accounting, then the authenticated
  link handshake reaching OPEN on both sides.
